00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.541
GABRIELA FERRARA: In this video,
we will share some good news

00:00:02.541 --> 00:00:03.060
with you.

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:06.270
We finally have
external primary server

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:08.910
that we will allow you
to migrate your MySQL

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:12.080
server to Google Cloud
Platform with minimal downtime.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:14.930
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:19.825 --> 00:00:22.100
We know there are two
ways to migrate data,

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:25.190
and this talk talks
about both but shows

00:00:25.190 --> 00:00:27.410
you the new feature,
the replication

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:31.990
for an external
primary intercloud SQL.

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:35.450
The traditional way involves
a variable downtime migration,

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:38.180
which could take from minutes
to hours or even weeks,

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:41.880
depending on the
size of your data.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:45.430
This approach usually have
the following configuration.

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:48.940
First, an instance of your
database, your primary,

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:51.410
needs to disconnect
from your application

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:53.570
so the data doesn't change.

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:56.950
Then, export this
data to a SQL file

00:00:56.950 --> 00:01:01.990
or CSV using tools like
MySQL DAP or PG-DAC.

00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:04.269
After that, provision
a new instance

00:01:04.269 --> 00:01:07.690
and import the file
exporter to this new server.

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:09.850
At this point, connect
to the application

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:12.760
again so the downtime is over.

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:14.920
If you can afford
the downtime, this

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:18.240
is the simplest way of
doing a database migration.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:20.660
If you can't afford
that much downtime,

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:23.560
there is a different
way using replication.

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:25.240
On this approach,
your application

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:28.420
is connecting to an
instance, your primary,

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:31.430
and all the changes are
being sent to a new instance,

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:32.940
a replica, elsewhere.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:35.020
Replication will
catch up, but you

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:37.540
need to stop the application
from continuing writing

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:40.330
to the existing instance.

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:43.420
Once that is completed, the
replication is also stopped,

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:47.530
and the new instance is
promoted to be the new primary.

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:49.450
With the promotion
process completed,

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:52.330
you can now point your
application to read and write

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:53.800
to the new instance.

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:55.750
Watch this session
and don't miss out

00:01:55.750 --> 00:01:57.320
this feature in action.

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:00.400
Watch as I migrate the database
from a single instance within

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:03.250
a common scenario-- running
a WordPress application with

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:05.320
Apache and MySQL--

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:08.620
to a new scenario where the
database is fully managed

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:11.380
by Cloud SQL, leaving
the previous instance to

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:14.110
only serve the application.

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:16.190
For more
in-the-trenches details,

00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:18.640
check out the full talk
and follow the steps.

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:21.310
Also, don't forget to subscribe
to our channel for more

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:22.630
great Next Rewind content.

00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:25.380
[MUSIC PLAYING]

